WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:06.244
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level four.

2
00:00:06.544 --> 00:00:10.047
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.347 --> 00:00:17.225
Unit five: Migration & Refugees. Today you will stress how severe a situation was with So / Such inversion.

4
00:00:17.525 --> 00:00:20.398
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.333 --> 00:00:24.566
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.417 --> 00:00:26.487
Migration.

7
00:00:27.483 --> 00:00:31.317
Migration means the movement of people from one place to live in another.

8
00:00:31.917 --> 00:00:34.102
Migration has shaped every continent.

9
00:00:34.717 --> 00:00:37.578
Your turn. Say: Migration.

10
00:00:40.433 --> 00:00:41.539
Migration.

11
00:00:42.283 --> 00:00:43.377
Refugee.

12
00:00:44.383 --> 00:00:48.357
Refugee means a person forced to leave their country to escape danger.

13
00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:51.124
The refugee crossed the river at night.

14
00:00:51.717 --> 00:00:54.799
Your turn. Say: Refugee.

15
00:00:57.633 --> 00:00:58.669
Refugee.

16
00:00:59.417 --> 00:01:00.801
Asylum seeker.

17
00:01:01.817 --> 00:01:05.767
Asylum seeker means a person who asks another country for protection.

18
00:01:06.383 --> 00:01:08.766
The asylum seeker waited for her interview.

19
00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:12.722
Your turn. Say: Asylum seeker.

20
00:01:15.567 --> 00:01:16.858
Asylum seeker.

21
00:01:17.617 --> 00:01:18.687
Displacement.

22
00:01:19.683 --> 00:01:24.191
Displacement means being forced to leave your home, usually by war or disaster.

23
00:01:24.783 --> 00:01:27.793
Displacement affected millions after the floods.

24
00:01:28.383 --> 00:01:31.291
Your turn. Say: Displacement.

25
00:01:34.133 --> 00:01:35.204
Displacement.

26
00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:37.729
Humanitarian crisis.

27
00:01:38.717 --> 00:01:43.804
Humanitarian crisis means a serious emergency in which many people lack basic needs.

28
00:01:44.417 --> 00:01:47.322
A humanitarian crisis followed the earthquake.

29
00:01:47.917 --> 00:01:51.626
Your turn. Say: Humanitarian crisis.

30
00:01:54.467 --> 00:01:56.304
Humanitarian crisis.

31
00:01:57.050 --> 00:01:58.190
Resettlement.

32
00:01:59.183 --> 00:02:03.575
Resettlement means moving refugees to a safe country where they can build a new life.

33
00:02:04.183 --> 00:02:06.659
Resettlement gave the family a fresh start.

34
00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:10.018
Your turn. Say: Resettlement.

35
00:02:12.867 --> 00:02:13.961
Resettlement.

36
00:02:14.717 --> 00:02:16.054
Border policy.

37
00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:20.791
Border policy means the rules about who may cross into a country.

38
00:02:21.383 --> 00:02:24.056
A strict border policy slowed arrivals.

39
00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:27.813
Your turn. Say: Border policy.

40
00:02:30.667 --> 00:02:32.167
Border policy.

41
00:02:32.917 --> 00:02:34.011
Integration.

42
00:02:35.017 --> 00:02:39.489
Integration means helping newcomers to become full members of a society.

43
00:02:40.083 --> 00:02:42.768
Integration requires language classes and work.

44
00:02:43.383 --> 00:02:46.291
Your turn. Say: Integration.

45
00:02:49.133 --> 00:02:50.529
Integration.

46
00:02:51.333 --> 00:02:54.914
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:55.750 --> 00:03:02.756
Visal, a reporter writes 'So severe was the humanitarian crisis that thousands fled.' Why is 'was' before 'the crisis'?

48
00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:16.954
After So + adjective at the start, we invert the verb and subject. The normal order is 'The crisis was so severe that thousands fled.' The inverted form sounds dramatic and formal.

49
00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:19.341
Does 'such' work too?

50
00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:31.824
Yes: 'Such was the scale of displacement that camps overflowed.' With such + be, the verb comes first. We use it to show the result of an extreme situation.

51
00:03:32.917 --> 00:03:34.498
Can we use it in speech?

52
00:03:35.583 --> 00:03:44.319
Rarely. It belongs to formal writing and speeches, where a writer wants to stress how serious the situation was.

53
00:03:45.467 --> 00:03:47.002
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:53.356
Grammar focus: Inversion: So plus adjective plus be plus subject plus that.

55
00:03:53.656 --> 00:03:55.028
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:55.717 --> 00:03:59.133
So severe was the crisis that thousands fled their homes.

57
00:04:00.017 --> 00:04:03.375
Such was the scale of displacement that camps overflowed.

58
00:04:04.283 --> 00:04:07.931
So strict is the border policy that few asylum seekers can enter.

59
00:04:08.867 --> 00:04:16.869
Begin with So + adjective or Such, then put the verb before the subject: So severe was the crisis that… It is formal and dramatic.

60
00:04:17.617 --> 00:04:21.500
Be careful! Don't say: So big the crisis was that all fled.

61
00:04:23.433 --> 00:04:26.211
Say: So big was the crisis that all fled.

62
00:04:26.511 --> 00:04:28.383
Put the verb before the subject.

63
00:04:29.167 --> 00:04:32.040
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:32.833 --> 00:04:33.939
People on the Move.

65
00:04:34.239 --> 00:04:39.571
Migration has always shaped the world, but forced displacement creates special urgency.

66
00:04:40.567 --> 00:04:44.656
So serious was the conflict that millions became refugees within a year.

67
00:04:45.667 --> 00:04:50.859
Such is the pressure on border policy that governments struggle to balance security and compassion.

68
00:04:51.867 --> 00:04:57.338
An asylum seeker may wait years for a decision, and resettlement offers only a few a new start.

69
00:04:58.333 --> 00:05:03.920
Successful integration, however, benefits both newcomers and the communities that welcome them.

70
00:05:05.033 --> 00:05:09.058
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:09.917 --> 00:05:13.124
So rapid blank the displacement that aid could not keep up.

72
00:05:13.424 --> 00:05:16.764
A, was. B, it was. C, did.

73
00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:22.323
The answer is A: was.

74
00:05:23.483 --> 00:05:26.714
A person who asks a country for protection is an blank.

75
00:05:27.014 --> 00:05:31.271
A, asylum seeker. B, investor. C, ambassador.

76
00:05:34.833 --> 00:05:37.146
The answer is A: asylum seeker.

77
00:05:38.283 --> 00:05:40.086
Choose the correct sentence.

78
00:05:40.386 --> 00:05:47.522
A, So large the camp was that it flooded.. B, So large was the camp that it flooded.. C, So large was that the camp flooded..

79
00:05:51.067 --> 00:05:54.692
The answer is B: So large was the camp that it flooded..

80
00:05:55.850 --> 00:05:59.220
Helping newcomers become part of society is called blank.

81
00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:03.823
A, deterrence. B, integration. C, inflation.

82
00:06:07.367 --> 00:06:09.273
The answer is B: integration.

83
00:06:10.417 --> 00:06:13.670
According to the reading, who benefits from integration?

84
00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:19.760
A, only newcomers. B, newcomers and host communities. C, only governments.

85
00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:27.076
The answer is B: newcomers and host communities.

86
00:06:28.267 --> 00:06:31.708
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:36.361
What challenges might a refugee face when resettling in a new country?

88
00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:46.592
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:46.892 --> 00:07:14.535
A refugee may face language barriers, difficulty finding work and trouble having qualifications recognised. Many also carry trauma from displacement and feel lonely far from family. Prejudice and complicated paperwork can make daily life harder. Good integration programmes, language classes and community support help newcomers rebuild their lives with dignity.

90
00:07:14.835 --> 00:07:16.486
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:17.383 --> 00:07:24.506
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and inversion: so plus adjective plus be plus subject plus that.

92
00:07:24.806 --> 00:07:28.944
Today's value is compassion. Care about the suffering of others and help.

93
00:07:29.244 --> 00:07:31.896
See you in Unit six. Goodbye!

94
00:07:32.196 --> 00:07:35.940
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

